  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Mild temperatures through next Thursday. \n\n-  Unsettled weather, with cooler temperatures by Friday into \n   next weekend.\n\nThe Northern Rockies will be under the influence of a split flow\nweather pattern through the middle of next week. Model guidance\nremains in strong agreement for a closed low to remain nearly\nstationary off the coast of California by Monday, with the storm\ntrack associated with the polar jet remaining across Alaska and\nnorthern Canada. This pattern will provide mild temperatures, with\nday-time highs running around 10 degrees above average across the\nNorthern Rockies. Most areas will remain dry, with isolated \nshowers across north-central Idaho into southwest Montana by \nTuesday and Wednesday as moisture spins northward from the low \nnear the California coast. \n\nForecast uncertainty begins to increase by Thursday into Friday.\nEnsemble model guidance continues to advertise a wide range of\nsolutions as the longwave trough over Alaska and Canada begins to\ndive southward towards the Western US by Thursday. The majority of \nmodels suggest the this trough will eventually phase with the low\nof the California coast, bringing increased chances for showers\nand precipitation by Friday. Details remain uncertain as the \ntrack and strength of these low pressure systems remains difficult\nto pin down. Nonetheless, by Friday into Saturday, around 90% of \nthe models suggest a cool down, with temperatures dropping 10-15 \ndegrees, bringing snow levels down near 4,000-5,500 feet.",
